-- title:
--   Rigidification numerator of the edge family at an arbitrary base point
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime, `Zp2 p` denotes $W(\mathbb F_{p^2})$, and `pIdeal p B` is the ideal of $B$ generated by $p$. A `FormalODModule p B` is a two‑dimensional commutative formal group law $F$ over $B$ together with an action of $W(\mathbb F_{p^2})$ by endomorphisms and an endomorphism $\varpi$ satisfying $\varpi\circ\varpi=[p]$ and $\varpi\circ a=\sigma(a)\circ\varpi$; `gradedPiece X j n` is the subgroup of the Cartier module $M_X$ of those $f$ with $[c]_X f=j([c])^{p^n}f$ for all $c\in\mathbb F_{p^2}$.
--
--   Data. Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$ and $\iota\colon W(\mathbb F_{p^2})\to W(k)$ a ring homomorphism; put $O=W(k)$, $\bar O=O/pO$ and let $\bar\jmath$ be $\iota$ followed by $O\to\bar O$. Let $\Phi$ be a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module over $\bar O$.
--
--   Hypotheses on $\Phi$ and its numerator. The hypothesis `hΦ` is that $\Phi$ is special for $\bar\jmath$, i.e. `lieZero` and `lieOne` of $\Phi$ are complementary submodules of the Lie module and both are invertible; `hΦ4` is that $\Phi$ has height $4$, i.e. the action of $p$ has kernel of degree $p^{4}$; `h0Φ` is the inclusion $\mathrm{lieZero}_{\bar\jmath}(\Phi)\subseteq\ker(\mathrm{lieVarpi}_\Phi)$; `hcΦ` is that the graded pieces of $M_\Phi$ in degrees $0$ and $1$ are complementary. Writing $D_\Phi$ for the graded Cartier module datum `Φ.toGradedCartierModuleData` attached to $\bar\jmath$ and `hcΦ`, an additive map $r_\Phi\colon(\mathrm{Fin}\,2\to\mathbb Z_p)\to N(D_\Phi)$ is given, subject to `hrΦ`: for every additive $L\colon D_\Phi.M\to N(D_\Phi)$ which is a canonical $L$-map in the sense of `IsCanonicalLMap`, $r_\Phi$ maps the whole of $\mathrm{Fin}\,2\to\mathbb Z_p$ bijectively onto the degree‑$0$ $\eta$-piece `etaPiece` of $L$.
--
--   A matrix $g\in\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb Q_p)$ with $g=\mathrm{diag}(p,1)$ is part of the data (`hg`).
--
--   The edge family. Let $E=$ `EdgeFamily.edgeRingCharP p` $\bar O$ be the edge chart ring (a localisation of an edge quotient ring of $\bar O$ at the discriminant, with distinguished elements $\xi,\eta$), and let $X$ be a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module over $E$ with $\gamma\colon\mathrm{Fin}\,2\to M_X$. The structure map for $X$ is $\iota$ followed by $O\to\bar O\to E$. The hypothesis `hγ` says that $\gamma$ is a homogeneous $V$-basis: $\gamma_i$ lies in the graded piece of degree $i$ and the matrix of tangents $(\mathrm{tangent}(\gamma_i)_k)$ has unit determinant; `hγa` says that $\gamma$ has the edge structure constants `EdgeFamily.edgeRingConstants` (the family $a_{m,i}$ equal to `branchConstants` of $\eta$ for $i=0$ and of $\xi$ for $i=1$), i.e. for each $i$ and each $N$ there is $h\in M_X$ with $\varpi\gamma_i=\sum_{m<N}V^{m}\bigl([a_{m,i}]\gamma_{\mathrm{piIndex}(m,i)}\bigr)+V^{N}h$; `hXs` says $X$ is special and `hX4` that $X$ has height $4$.
--
--   The node point and the node isogeny. A ring homomorphism $f_0\colon E\to\bar O$ is given which splits the structure map ($f_0\circ\mathrm{alg}=\mathrm{id}$, hypothesis `hf₀`) and kills both $\xi$ and $\eta$ (`hf₀ξ`, `hf₀η`). Further data are $m\in\mathbb N$ and $\rho_0\in$ `Series` $\bar O$ with `hρ₀`: $\rho_0$ is an isogeny of height $4m$ from $\Phi$ to $X\otimes_{f_0}\bar O$, that is, a homomorphism of formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules whose kernel has degree $p^{4m}$. Let $t_0$ be the rigidified datum over $\bar O$ consisting of $X\otimes_{f_0}\bar O$, the integer $m$ and the reduction of $\rho_0$ modulo $p\bar O$. The hypotheses `hOD₀`, `hcb` and `hcΦg` assert respectively that the series `Rigidified.ρ` of $t_0$ is a homomorphism of formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules from `Rigidified.Φbar` (the base change of $\Phi$ determined by $O\to\bar O$) to `Rigidified.Xbar` of $t_0$; that for the identity base change of $\bar O$ the graded pieces of degrees $0$ and $1$ of $(t_0)$`.XbarS` are complementary; and that the same holds for `PhibarS`.
--
--   Normalisation of the node numerator. With $a\in\mathbb N$, the hypothesis `hN` states, for both $i\in\mathrm{Fin}\,2$, that
--   $$p^{a}\cdot \mathrm{rigidNum}(\iota,\mathrm{hc}\Phi,r_\Phi,\,O\to\bar O,\,t_0,\mathrm{hOD}_0,\mathrm{id},\mathrm{hcb},\mathrm{hc}\Phi g)(e_i)=p^{a+m}\cdot v_i,$$
--   where $e_i=$ `Pi.single i 1`, where `rigidNum` is by definition the composite of $r_\Phi$ with the map on $N$-modules induced by the Cartier base change $M_\Phi\to M_{\mathrm{PhibarS}}$ and then with the map induced by pushforward along the reduced $\rho$, and where $v_0=\mathrm{nMk}(b_0(\gamma_0),0)$ and $v_1=\mathrm{nVarpi}\bigl(\mathrm{nMk}(b_0(\gamma_1),0)\bigr)$, with $b_0$ the Cartier module base change along $f_0$, then along $\bar O\to\bar O/p\bar O$, then along the induced map of the identity of $\bar O$.
--
--   The edge isogeny. A morphism $\rho_1$ of formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules over $E$ from $(X\otimes_{f_0}\bar O)\otimes_{\bar O}E$ to $X$ is given, with `hρ₁0` and `hρ₁1` prescribing its effect on the base changes of the $V$-basis:
--   $$\rho_{1*}(\gamma_0)=p\,\gamma_0-V\bigl([\eta^{p^2}]\gamma_1\bigr),\qquad \rho_{1*}(\gamma_1)=p\,\gamma_1-V\bigl([\xi^{p^2}]\gamma_0\bigr),$$
--   where on the left the arguments are the images of $\gamma_0,\gamma_1$ under base change along $f_0$ and then along $\bar O\to E$, $[\,\cdot\,]$ is the Teichmüller lift and $V$ is `verschiebungInt`; and `hρ₁h` asserts that $\rho_1$ is an isogeny of height $4$.
--
--   Base change data. Let $S$ be a commutative ring, $y\colon E\to S$ a ring homomorphism, $S'$ a commutative ring and $g_S\colon S\to S'$ a ring homomorphism; write $\psi$ for the composite $O\to\bar O\to E\to S$. Let $t$ be the image under $y$ of the rigidified datum over $E$ consisting of $X$, the integer $m+1$ and the composite of the reduction of $\rho_1$ modulo $pE$ with the image of $\rho_0$ under $\bar O\to E\to E/pE$; thus $t$ has underlying module $X\otimes_yS$, level $m+1$, and series the image of that composite under the induced map $E/pE\to S/pS$. The hypotheses `hOD'`, `hcb'` and `hcΦg'` are the analogues over $S$ of `hOD₀`, `hcb`, `hcΦg` for $\psi$ and the further base change $g_S$: the series of $t$ is a homomorphism of formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules from `Rigidified.Φbar` of $\psi$ to `Rigidified.Xbar` of $t$, and the graded pieces of degrees $0$ and $1$ are complementary for $(t)$`.XbarS` $g_S$ and for `PhibarS` $\psi$ $g_S$.
--
--   Conclusion. For both $i\in\mathrm{Fin}\,2$, in the $N$-module of the graded Cartier module datum of $(t)$`.XbarS` $g_S$ relative to `jSbar` $\iota\,\psi\,g_S$ and `hcb'`,
--   $$p^{a}\cdot\mathrm{rigidNum}(\iota,\mathrm{hc}\Phi,r_\Phi,\psi,t,\mathrm{hOD}',g_S,\mathrm{hcb}',\mathrm{hc}\Phi g')(e_i)=p^{a+m}\cdot w_i,$$
--   where $e_i=$ `Pi.single i 1` and, with $b$ denoting the Cartier module base change along $y$, then along $S\to S/pS$, then along the map $S/pS\to S'/pS'$ induced by $g_S$,
--   $$w_0=\mathrm{nMk}\bigl(b\bigl(p\,\gamma_0-V([\eta^{p^2}]\gamma_1)\bigr),\,0\bigr),\qquad w_1=\mathrm{nVarpi}\Bigl(\mathrm{nMk}\bigl(b\bigl(p\,\gamma_1-V([\xi^{p^2}]\gamma_0)\bigr),\,0\bigr)\Bigr).$$
--   The right-hand sides are written out by the explicit formulas of `hρ₁0` and `hρ₁1` rather than through $\rho_1$, and the power of $p$ on the right is $p^{a+m}$, the same exponent as in the normalisation `hN`, although the level of $t$ is $m+1$.
--
--   This is the step of the Čerednik–Drinfeld part of the formalisation that transports the normalisation of the rigidification numerator from the node point of the edge family across one explicit edge isogeny, and then to an arbitrary base point: the numerator of the rigidified datum $(X,m+1,\bar\rho_1\circ\bar\rho_0)$ read over any $S'$ is pinned, up to the factor $p^{a+m}$, to the images of $p\gamma_0-V([\eta^{p^2}]\gamma_1)$ and $p\gamma_1-V([\xi^{p^2}]\gamma_0)$. It is the common ingredient of the three existence results for $\eta$-sections with prescribed tangent on the edge family ([`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.exists_isEtaSection_one_tangent_eq_neg_mul_of_edge_isogeny_of_apply_xi_ne_zero`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.exists_isEtaSection_one_tangent_eq_neg_mul_of_edge_isogeny_of_apply_xi_ne_zero) and its companions).
